---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Contact ShouldISpend"
  description="Contact Should I Spend with corrections, calculator feedback, editorial questions, and site issues."
>
  <main class="contact-page">
    <p class="eyebrow">Contact</p>

    <h1>Contact Should I Spend</h1>

    <p class="updated">Last reviewed: July 2026</p>

    <p class="intro">
      ShouldISpend is built around practical financial decision tools. Reader
      feedback helps improve calculator explanations, assumptions, examples,
      page clarity, and coverage of real-world spending situations.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Who Operates ShouldISpend</h2>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend is independently operated by Dustin Baker, a longtime
        digital publisher, writer, and editor. The site is focused on
        educational spending-pressure tools, plain-language financial context,
        and practical calculator feedback rather than individualized financial
        advice.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="contact-card">
      <h2>Email</h2>

      <p>
        For corrections, calculator feedback, topic suggestions, partnership
        questions, or general inquiries, email:
      </p>

      <p class="email-line">
        <a href="mailto:hello@shouldispend.com">hello@shouldispend.com</a>
      </p>

      <p>
        Please include the page URL when your message is about a specific
        calculator, guide, result, broken link, typo, or unclear explanation.
        That makes it much easier to review the issue accurately.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What to Send</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>Calculator behavior that seems confusing or inconsistent</li>
        <li>Suggestions for new spending-pressure calculators</li>
        <li>Broken links, formatting problems, or unclear page sections</li>
        <li>Questions about the site's methodology or assumptions</li>
        <li>Corrections involving wording, examples, or outdated information</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What ShouldISpend Cannot Do</h2>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend cannot provide personalized financial, legal, tax,
        insurance, medical, investment, or debt advice by email. The site also
        cannot review private financial documents, guarantee calculator
        outcomes, or tell a user exactly what decision to make.
      </p>

      <p>
        Calculator results and email responses, if any, should be treated as
        educational information only. For personal advice, users should consult
        qualified professionals who can review their full situation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Response Expectations</h2>

      <p>
        Messages are reviewed as site feedback when possible, but ShouldISpend
        cannot guarantee a response to every inquiry. Reports about broken
        calculators, confusing results, incorrect links, or unclear assumptions
        are especially helpful because they can directly improve the site for
        future readers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Helpful Links</h2>

      <p>
        Before reaching out, readers may also want to review the
        <a href="/about/">About</a> page,
        <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>,
        <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>, and
        <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <style>
    .contact-page {
      max-width: 860px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 72px 24px;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      color: #0f766e;
      font-weight: 800;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.4rem);
      line-height: 1;
      margin: 0 0 24px;
      color: #061126;
    }

    h2 {
      margin-top: 42px;
      margin-bottom: 14px;
      color: #061126;
      font-size: 1.75rem;
      line-height: 1.2;
    }

    p,
    li {
      color: #475569;
      line-height: 1.8;
      font-size: 1.05rem;
    }

    p {
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    ul {
      padding-left: 1.4rem;
    }

    li {
      margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    a {
      color: #0f766e;
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .intro {
      font-size: 1.16rem;
      color: #334155;
    }

    .contact-card {
      background: #f8fafc;
      border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;
      border-radius: 22px;
      padding: 26px;
      margin: 34px 0;
    }

    .contact-card h2 {
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    .email-line {
      font-size: 1.18rem;
      margin: 10px 0 18px;
    }

    .trust-closeout-section {
      margin-top: 32px;
      padding-top: 28px;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    }

    .trust-closeout-section h2 {
      color: var(--text);
      margin-bottom: 14px;
    }

    .trust-closeout-section p {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.75;
      margin: 0 0 16px;
    }

  </style>

      <section id="TrustContactCloseout" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>What to send</h2>
        <p>
          Contact is best used for calculator feedback, corrections, broken links,
          unclear wording, accessibility issues, and suggestions for improving an
          existing page. Helpful messages include the page URL, the numbers entered, the
          result that seemed confusing, and the reason the estimate did not match the
          real-world decision.
        </p>
        <p>
          Should I Spend can review site issues and editorial feedback, but it cannot
          provide personal financial advice, legal advice, tax advice, medical advice,
          insurance advice, or emergency help. Do not send account numbers, Social
          Security numbers, bank information, private medical records, passwords, or
          other sensitive personal data.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Corrections and calculator feedback</h2>
        <p>
          If a calculator appears to miss a cost category, use outdated wording, or
          explain a decision unclearly, include the page and the specific issue. The
          most useful feedback explains what should be clarified: a missing fee, a lease
          detail, a timing issue, a required deposit, a recurring cost, or a scenario
          where the current result could mislead a reader.
        </p>
        <p>
          Feedback is used to improve the public page. A response is not guaranteed, and
          messages should not be treated as a private advisory relationship.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Before acting on a calculator result</h2>
        <p>
          Readers should verify the final numbers with the business, school, landlord,
          provider, lender, employer, insurer, or professional involved in the decision.
          Calculator results are planning tools, and real quotes, contracts, bills, and
          policies control the final cost.
        </p>
      </section>


      <section id="TrustContactCloseoutResponse" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>How feedback is reviewed</h2>
        <p>
          Feedback is most helpful when it identifies a real reader problem: an input
          that needs explanation, a cost that should be named, a link that should be
          clearer, or a limitation that should be easier to find. Site feedback is used
          to improve public pages and calculator guidance, not to provide individual
          advice through email.
        </p>
      </section>


      <section id="TrustContactCloseoutUsefulMessage" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>What makes a message useful</h2>
        <p>
          A useful message points to a specific page and explains what was confusing or
          incomplete. For example, a reader might say that a moving calculator should
          mention storage access hours, a rent calculator should make utility timing
          clearer, or a funeral calculator should separate required costs from optional
          upgrades. Specific examples make it easier to improve the page for everyone.
        </p>
        <p>
          General messages are still welcome, but they may be harder to act on. The site
          cannot evaluate a private budget or approve a personal spending decision by
          email. The best contact messages help identify a public-page improvement:
          clearer wording, better examples, a missing limitation, a broken link, or a
          cost category that should be explained before a reader spends money.
        </p>
      </section>

      </Layout>
